Firstmate fork: progress-stall guard
===================================

Registers the fork-owned source that wakes Firstmate when a worker looks busy
but has stopped producing anything: a task that is still declaring `working`
while its status file, its recorded worktree, and its data directory have all
been silent past a threshold window. That wake closes the gap where a pane
stays legitimately active - so no existing supervision alarms - while the run
behind it goes nowhere, a class of stall that today is only caught when the
captain asks what is taking so long, or by a per-task check written by hand
for one job. This source is one standing guard for any task and any harness:
no per-task wiring, no poll to run by hand.

It is fork-owned. Everything lives under fork/progress-guard/, upstream code
stays byte-identical (no hook block is needed), and it reaches Firstmate
through the same documented extension seam the PR-readiness wake uses: an
external process-event adapter, registered in the home, that emits one
ordinary process-event wake which Firstmate's existing supervision already
consumes.

Like fork/README and fork/pr-green/README, this file is plain text with no
extension on purpose: upstream's documentation-audience check requires every
tracked *.md, *.mdx, *.rst, and *.txt file to be registered in an
upstream-owned inventory, and the fork does not edit that inventory.


Advisory by construction
------------------------

The wake this source emits is advisory: it says "look at this worker", never
"the worker is stuck" as a verdict and never an instruction to act. The
constraints below bind the predicate installed at the seam, and the shipped
code keeps them.

  - Nothing here steers a worker, answers a finding, changes a task record,
    interrupts or relaunches anything, or touches any lifecycle, merge, or
    supervision authority. The only file it ever writes is its own latch
    marker (see "One wake per stall: the latch").
  - Silence is the default for the stall judgment: an unreadable status file,
    a task whose worktree cannot be attributed, an evidence walk that fails or
    runs out of its bounded budget, a status line that changes between the
    gate's read and delivery, or any doubt must produce no wake, never a guess
    that a worker is stuck. A guard that cries wolf is worse than no guard.
  - A failure of the adapter's own read is not stall evidence: it is captured
    as host failure evidence and classified apart from the advisory body (see
    "Event delivery and acknowledgement"), never as a stalled worker.


Supported scope
---------------

  Tasks        Any task of this home whose record state/<id>.meta exists,
               whose LAST status line carries the exact `working` verb, whose
               record carries a usable spawn_gen= incarnation, and whose
               worktree is attributable: a worktree= path that is absolute and
               currently an ordinary directory, without a secondmate-home
               marker inside it. There is no delivery-mode, yolo, kind, or
               harness gate: a stall is a stall whatever the task ships with.
  Refused      A secondmate record (kind=secondmate or mode=secondmate) is
               never eligible: a secondmate home runs its own supervision
               inside the worktree, so its tree churns whether or not the mate
               is producing anything and the evidence would be meaningless.
               So are a record with no usable spawn_gen=, a record with no
               worktree= or a worktree that is gone (attribution failure - the
               source stays silent rather than guess), a task with no status
               file, and any task whose last status line is not `working`: a
               declared `paused:` wait with a resume condition, a `blocked:`
               or `needs-decision:` line, a terminal `done:` or `failed:`
               line, and trailing continuation prose are all silence however
               long they wait.
  Home         Each home reads only its own state, data, and recorded
               worktrees: a secondmate home that wants this capability
               registers its own source against its own home, and no home ever
               evaluates another home's records.
  Records      A record that is not a plain regular file, that carries a
               duplicate key, that cannot be read, or whose task id is not
               path-safe is skipped silently.


Signals and the stall threshold
-------------------------------

A candidate task stalls only when ALL THREE evidence sources have been silent
for the whole threshold window:

  status       no new line in state/<id>.status (the file's own mtime).
  worktree     no new or modified file or directory under the recorded
               worktree, through a git status-free mtime walk: .git is never
               opened (a supervisor's own read-only git commands can never
               make the walk self-fulfilling), symlinks are counted but never
               followed, the walk is bounded to six levels of depth and to its
               share of one wall-clock budget, and the same generated trees
               bin/fm-classify-lib.sh prunes (node_modules, venv, target,
               dist, and the rest) are skipped so a package tree cannot eat
               the budget. Directories count as evidence because a file
               created and deleted between two polls still moves its parent
               directory's mtime.
  data         no new file or directory under data/<id>/. A task with no data
               directory simply has no evidence there; a data directory that
               exists but cannot be read is doubt, not silence.

The newest mtime across those sources is the task's newest evidence, and
now minus that newest evidence is the observed silence; when the silence
reaches the threshold the task is stalled. The threshold is
state/progress-guard/stall-threshold-secs, 1500 seconds (25 minutes) by
default, and that file is the one place a home tunes the window (range
1..86400; a malformed knob falls back to the default rather than lengthening
or disabling it).

Verb classification belongs to bin/fm-classify-lib.sh, and this source reads
the last status line's verb through a re-expression of its status_line_verb
(text before the first colon, cut at the first bracket, trimmed, correlation
tokens dropped after the first word). The reading is strictly conservative
against the library's own last-event scan: the library may skip a trailing
line this source treats as the last line (a missed wake, the safe direction),
never the reverse, because a trailing line that parses as `working` is always
an event for the library too. Only an exact `working` verb is ever a
candidate, so a `paused:` declaration can never alarm regardless of how the
threshold is set.


One wake per stall: the latch
-----------------------------

On a stall the source emits exactly one wake naming the task, the observed
silence, and the newest evidence it could find, then latches per (task,
latest status line) so the same stall wakes once. The latch is one marker per
task, <home>/state/<id>.progress-guard-latch, written as one fixed-arity line
record and published by temp-file rename (0600, same directory, fsynced):

  marker     version line fm-fork-progress-guard-v1, then task id, the task
             record's spawn_gen= incarnation, the sha256 of the latest status
             line the wake was about, the host request id that produced the
             wake, and the epoch second it was published.
  latches    a marker suppresses when its incarnation and status-line hash
             equal the ones the gate just read: the same task, worker, and
             latest status line is one stall and wakes once. A new status
             line or a new spawn_gen (a relaunch) re-arms the question.
  caps       a re-armed question is still suppressed while now minus the
             marker's epoch is under state/progress-guard/
             min-wake-interval-secs (3600 by default), so a task never gets
             more than one wake per hour no matter how the threshold, a
             changed status line, or a relaunch interact (range 0..86400; 0
             disables the cap, which only a test or a deliberate operator
             choice asks for).
  repeat     two things make the latch fail toward one possible duplicate
             wake and never toward suppression. (1) A marker write that
             fails, is interrupted, or is unreadable afterwards leaves no
             valid marker, so the next poll answers again. (2) The marker
             records the host request id that produced its wake, and core
             derives that id from the next uncaptured result sequence -
             unchanged while a wake has not been captured, new once it has
             been. A retry after a crash between answering and capture
             arrives with the same id as the marker and is answered again,
             while an ordinary later poll arrives with a different id and is
             silent.

The marker is written before the wake is returned, never after: a crash in
between is repaired by the request-id rule above. A marker whose identity no
longer matches still holds the hourly cap, because the cap is the hard bound;
pruning drops a marker only when the task record itself is gone or the marker
file is malformed, so a relaunch or a changed status line never resets the
hour. Pruning runs on every poll, so a torn-down task leaves nothing behind;
there is no separate teardown command because the marker is inert state that
names only its own task and the next poll after a teardown removes it.


Registration
------------

Registration is an operator step; this repository change activates nothing.
The package must live outside every Git project, as upstream requires for any
bound package. With FM_HOME exported as the home the source belongs to:

  mkdir -p "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages"
  cp -R fork/progress-guard/extension "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/progress-guard"
  bin/fm-extension.sh bind "$HOME/.local/share/firstmate-packages/progress-guard" \
    --adapter progress-guard --trust-same-user-code \
    --consent task-metadata
  bin/fm-procevent.sh register-extension progress-guard progress-guard \
    --config-ref "home:$FM_HOME"

The one consent is honest rather than sandboxing: the package reads task
records, status files, worktrees, and data directories of the registered home
(task-metadata). It never opens a network connection, never reads a
credential, and requests neither consent. The config reference is the
private, non-secret statement of which home the source belongs to; it appears
in envelopes and registrations and never holds a credential.

`register-extension` prints the owner token and the exact retirement command.
Keep the token. To change the package, withdraw and re-bind:

  bin/fm-procevent.sh retire progress-guard --if-owner <owner-token>
  bin/fm-extension.sh retire-binding org.firstmate.fork.progress-guard \
    --if-binding-digest <binding-digest>
  (then bind and register the new copy again)

A registered source keeps Firstmate's supervision running and the watcher
restarts it every cycle, so retire the source to stop it. Before retiring for
good, drop the latch markers with the same shape of cleanup the poll already
runs: `rm -f "$FM_HOME"/state/*.progress-guard-latch` (they are inert, but a
home that goes away should leave none).


Event delivery and acknowledgement
----------------------------------

When the seam reports stalled tasks, the adapter returns one result carrying
an advisory block for each of them - never only the task whose id sorts
first - up to the result-size limit under "Known limits". Core captures it as
external evidence and publishes the ordinary bounded process-event wake, so
nothing new is introduced into Firstmate's supervision. The path is the one
the fork tests drive end to end:

  check: procevent progress-guard progress-guard <sequence>

  classify    bin/fm-procevent.sh classify <result-file> prints
              stall-advisory for a stall body and stall-error for a host
              failure record, so a failed read is never presented as stall
              evidence and stall evidence is never read as an instruction or
              a decision.
  terminal    no. The source never concludes itself, so it keeps listening
              for the next stall.
  silent      exactly two contents are recorded handled without a wake:
              (1) the host's own timeout evidence on this source: a captured
              firstmate.process-event-extension-error.v1 record with code
              timeout and operation source.poll under this extension's id is
              a poll the host stopped waiting for - its invoke timer counts
              time the machine spends asleep - so there is no stall content
              to wake about; (2) a stall capture whose every advisory is
              already concluded when the capture is read: each advisory's
              task record state/<id>.meta is gone, or its last status line
              carries a terminal `done` or `failed` verb. The question is
              asked at read time, never cached at capture time, so a task
              that finishes between capture and read never resurrects a moot
              wake.
              Both absorbs are safe because the source is advisory by
              construction: a lost poll is re-run by the next launch, the
              self-conclusion demands positive proof and never guesses (a
              live task, an unreadable status file, any other verb, a missing
              or unusable home reference, or a capture with any non-advisory
              leading line all keep the wake), a capture with several
              advisories concludes only when every one of them does, so a
              still-live task keeps the whole capture announced, and the
              latch can only repeat a wake, never swallow one.
              Every other result - a stall advisory for a live task, a
              non-timeout host failure, or a timeout naming another
              operation - keeps its wake and is re-announced until firstmate
              acknowledges it, so a single wake is never lost to a missed
              turn. That retry is per result sequence, not per task.
  acknowledge bin/fm-procevent.sh handled progress-guard <sequence>

The latch lives in the seam, not in core, so a re-announced result and a
re-polled stall stay different things: the first is core's ordinary retry of
one sequence until `handled`, the second is a poll the seam answers with
silence because that stall is already recorded. Neither is wake-queue
deduplication, and neither may stand in for the marker described under "One
wake per stall: the latch".


Latency and cost
----------------

The source holds one bounded long poll (up to 50 seconds) and re-scans the
home's task records every 5 seconds inside it, so a newly stalled task is
picked up mid-poll rather than at the next cycle. Each eligible task's
evidence walk happens at most once per poll per latest status line, inside
one shared 20-second scan budget for the poll with a 10-second per-task cap,
so one large worktree spends only its own share and a walk that runs out of
budget is silence for that task in that poll, never a stall verdict. When the
poll returns no result the runner exits and the ordinary supervision cycle
restarts it (FM_POLL, 15 seconds by default), so a stall is normally announced
within roughly one poll plus one cycle after the threshold passes.

Tests set state/progress-guard/poll-wait-ms (100-50000) to keep those waits
short; an operator may shorten it too, and it can never lengthen a wait.


Adapter state
-------------

The fork's own adapter state belongs to the seam. It writes exactly one
thing: the latch marker <home>/state/<id>.progress-guard-latch, described
under "One wake per stall: the latch", which the ordinary poll also prunes.
Everything else it reads belongs to upstream or to optional configuration it
never writes: state/<id>.meta, state/<id>.status, the recorded worktree,
data/<id>/, and the two knobs under state/progress-guard/. It never writes a
task record, a status line, or any authority record.


Files
-----

  extension/firstmate-extension.json  the process-event-adapter/1 manifest:
                                      id org.firstmate.fork.progress-guard,
                                      adapter name "progress-guard", consent
                                      task-metadata only.
  extension/fm-progress-guard.mjs     the adapter: config_ref validation, the
                                      scope gate above, the bounded long
                                      poll, and the protocol verbs.
  extension/stall.mjs                 the stall seam. It is the whole of the
                                      adapter's judgment: the three-source
                                      evidence scan, the stall predicate and
                                      threshold, the latch and its hourly cap,
                                      the wake body, and the self-conclusion
                                      of an already-captured advisory.


Tests
-----

  fork/tests/fm-fork-progress-guard.test.sh
                                      the scope gate against a fixture home
                                      (including the paused, terminal,
                                      continuation-prose, secondmate, and
                                      unattributable-worktree refusals), a
                                      malformed or unusable config_ref and the
                                      classification of the failure it
                                      captures, a working task with fresh
                                      evidence staying silent, and the real
                                      binding, registration, capture, wake,
                                      classification, re-announcement, and
                                      acknowledgement path for one stalled
                                      task.
  fork/tests/fm-fork-progress-guard-latch.test.sh
                                      the latch's own suite: a declared
                                      `paused:` task staying silent however
                                      long its evidence has been aged, one
                                      stall waking once with a second sweep
                                      silent, the hourly cap holding a
                                      re-armed question, a new status line
                                      re-arming the wake once the cap allows,
                                      and the same-request-id repeat of an
                                      uncaptured answer.
  fork/tests/fm-fork-progress-guard-self-conclude.test.sh
                                      the self-conclusion's own suite: a
                                      capture whose task record is gone, and
                                      one whose last status line is terminal,
                                      both recorded handled with no wake
                                      through the real host and runner; a
                                      conclusion landing between capture and
                                      read concluding the capture at read
                                      time; a live task's capture still
                                      re-announcing until acknowledged; and
                                      the side verdicts (live record, missing
                                      home reference, non-advisory content,
                                      the poll-timeout absorb, and a
                                      multi-advisory capture with one live
                                      task) all keeping their wake.

Every case drives the real extension host, the real process-event runner, and
real upstream task records against fixture homes; no test contacts a network,
a live fleet, or the running session's own home.


Known limits
------------

  - Deliberately undetected rather than guessed at: secondmate tasks (their
    homes churn on their own supervision, so the evidence is meaningless);
    tasks with no recorded worktree or a worktree that is gone (attribution
    failure, which stays silent by design); a status file whose last line is
    continuation prose after a `working` line (the strict last-line reading
    keeps the source silent, missing that stall rather than risking a false
    wake); activity confined entirely to a pruned tree (node_modules, venv,
    target, dist, and the rest) or deeper than six levels; and a walk that
    spends its whole wall-clock budget, which is inconclusive silence for
    that poll rather than a verdict.
  - The evidence is file mtimes, so a worker whose entire output for the
    window is CPU work with no file or status write at all looks identical to
    a stalled one; the wake is advisory evidence to look, and firstmate's own
    read of the worker decides what happened.
  - The verb reading honors the default vocabulary only: the extension host
    hands a bound package a minimal environment, so an FM_CAPTAIN_RE or
    FM_CLASSIFY_PAUSED_VERB override set in a watcher's environment is not
    visible here. That only ever widens silence (an override line parses as
    itself, not as `working`), never a wake.
  - The hourly cap reads its marker epoch against the poll's clock, so a
    backwards system clock can extend the cap until the clock catches up;
    that costs a late wake, never a duplicate one.
  - The latch needs the host's own request id to prove delivery. A consult
    without one neither reads nor writes a marker, so it can only ever repeat
    a wake. The real host always sends the id; only a test or a foreign
    caller reaches this case.
  - One result carries at most 30000 bytes of advisory blocks. A home with
    more simultaneously stalled tasks than fit leaves the ones past the bound
    out of that result instead of publishing a result the host would refuse.
    The seam latches a task when it returns its advisory, before the adapter
    measures the body, so a task dropped past that bound is latched like the
    rest and is re-offered only when its status line changes or its worker is
    replaced: a missed wake that needs dozens of simultaneously stalled
    tasks to reach, and a miss is the safe direction.
  - Two local homes each maintain their own registration, source, and
    markers; neither evaluates the other's records.
  - Nothing here has been exercised against a live fleet: the tests run the
    real extension host, the real process-event runner, the real stall seam,
    and real upstream task records against fixture homes with no network.
